---
<Layout
  title={seo.title}
  description={seo.description}
  keywords="cas statement, consolidated account statement, what is cas statement, cas statement explained, cas pdf, cas from cams, cas from kfintech, cas mutual fund, cas statement download"
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={{
    items: [
      { name: "Home", url: "https://arthavi.com/" },
      { name: "Guides", url: "https://arthavi.com/guides/cas-statement-explained/" },
      { name: "CAS Statement Explained", url: "https://arthavi.com/guides/cas-statement-explained/" }
    ]
  }}
>

<main class="container">
  <section class="page-header fade-in" style="text-align: center; padding: 4rem 0 1.5rem; margin-top: 31px;">
    <h1>CAS Statement Explained — What It Is and How to Use It</h1>
  </section>

  <div class="aeo-answer-block fade-in" style="max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto 2rem; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 16px; background: rgba(99,102,241,0.08); border: 1px solid rgba(99,102,241,0.2);">
    <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-body);">
      <strong style="color: var(--text-head);">A CAS (Consolidated Account Statement) is an official SEBI document from CAMS or KFintech that contains your complete mutual fund transaction history across all AMCs.</strong>
      Upload it to a portfolio tracker like Arthavi to instantly see your holdings, XIRR, and net worth — with no broker login and no email scraping required.
    </p>
  </div>

  <article class="prose fade-in delay-100" style="max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto;">

    <h2>What does a CAS statement contain?</h2>
    <p>Your CAS includes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Folio numbers</strong> for each AMC (e.g., HDFC, SBI, Axis, PPFAS)</li>
      <li><strong>Complete transaction history</strong> — every SIP, lump sum, redemption, switch, and dividend</li>
      <li><strong>NAV and units</strong> for each transaction</li>
      <li><strong>Current valuation</strong> as of the statement date</li>
      <li><strong>PAN-level identification</strong> linking all your folios</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is why CAS is the gold standard for portfolio import — it's comprehensive, official, and contains everything a tracker needs to show your complete mutual fund portfolio.</p>

    <h2>How is CAS different from individual AMC statements?</h2>
    <p>Individual AMC statements (from HDFC MF, SBI MF, etc.) only show that AMC's funds. A CAS <strong>consolidates all AMCs</strong> into a single document. One CAS upload to <a href="https://app.arthavi.com/register">Arthavi</a> replaces 10+ individual AMC imports.</p>

    <h2>How to download your CAS statement</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Visit CAMS or KFintech</strong> — Go to <a href="https://www.camsonline.com/Investors/Statements/Consolidated-Account-Statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">camsonline.com</a> or <a href="https://mfs.kfintech.com/investor/General/ConsolidatedAccountStatement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kfintech.com</a></li>
      <li><strong>Enter your email and PAN</strong> — Use the email registered with your mutual fund folios</li>
      <li><strong>Select "Detailed" statement</strong> — Choose the detailed/transaction-level option, not just summary</li>
      <li><strong>Receive via email</strong> — The PDF arrives in minutes, password-protected with your PAN</li>
      <li><strong>Upload to Arthavi</strong> — Import the PDF for instant portfolio tracking</li>
    </ol>
    <p>For step-by-step screenshots, see: <a href="/guides/how-to-import-cas-cams-kfintech-nsdl/">How to import CAS from CAMS, KFintech, and NSDL</a>.</p>

    <h2>What is the difference between CAS from CAMS and CAS from KFintech?</h2>
    <p>CAMS and KFintech are the two Registrar and Transfer Agents (RTAs) in India. Each covers different AMCs. However, <strong>both can generate a consolidated CAS</strong> that includes funds from all AMCs — they share data via SEBI mandate. So you only need to download from one source.</p>

    <h2>Is CAS safe to upload to a portfolio tracker?</h2>
    <p>Yes. A CAS is a <strong>read-only document</strong> — it does not contain your bank account details, trading passwords, or demat credentials. Uploading it to a trusted tracker like Arthavi (which processes it securely in your browser) is far safer than sharing broker login credentials or granting Gmail read access.</p>

    <h2>What is the password for CAS PDF?</h2>
    <p>The CAS PDF is password-protected with your <strong>PAN number in uppercase</strong>. For example, if your PAN is ABCDE1234F, enter that as the PDF password. Arthavi decrypts the PDF locally in your browser — the password never reaches our servers.</p>

    <div style="text-align: center; margin: 3rem 0;">
      <a href="https://app.arthavi.com/demo" class="btn btn-demo" style="padding: 1rem 2.5rem; font-size: 1.1rem;">
        Try Arthavi Demo — No Signup Required
      </a>
      <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--text-muted);">No broker login. No spam calls. Your data stays yours.</p>
    </div>

    <section id="faq" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>What is a CAS statement?</h3>
      <p>A Consolidated Account Statement (CAS) is an official SEBI document from CAMS or KFintech with your complete mutual fund history across all AMCs.</p>
      <h3>What is the CAS PDF password?</h3>
      <p>Your PAN number in uppercase (e.g., ABCDE1234F).</p>
      <h3>Is CAS safe to share with a portfolio tracker?</h3>
      <p>Yes. CAS is read-only and doesn't contain bank/trading credentials. Arthavi processes it in your browser.</p>
      <h3>How often should I download CAS?</h3>
      <p>Once a month or after new investments. SEBI mandates monthly CAS for active investors.</p>
      <h3>Do I need CAS from both CAMS and KFintech?</h3>
      <p>No. Either source generates a consolidated view covering all AMCs.</p>
    </section>

    <nav style="margin-top: 3rem; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 16px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);">
      <h3 style="font-size: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Related guides</h3>
